<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of the Dead</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1550–50 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Spells of Coming Forth by Day, Totenbuch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of the Dead is an ancient Egyptian funerary text, generally written on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and used from the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, around 1550 BCE, to around 50 BCE. It is a loose collection of magic spells meant to help the dead through the Duat, the underworld, and into the afterlife; its Egyptian name is translated as Spells of Coming Forth by Day. Some spells were drawn from the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> and Coffin Texts. There was no single canonical version, and the papyri, such as the Papyrus of Ani in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, vary in their spells and illustrations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-the-dead.jpg" alt="Book of the Dead" width="330" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hunefer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_pesado_del_corazón_en_el_Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the beginning of the New Kingdom, around 1550 BCE, the dead were buried with the <b>Book of the Dead</b>, or “Spells for Going Forth by Day”, a collection of nearly two hundred spells written on papyrus and often beautifully illustrated, such as the papyrus of the scribe…</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian texts, from the Pyramid Texts and the <b>Book of the Dead</b> to tales, letters and accounts, give voice to one of the longest-lasting civilisations in history and make it possible to know its people in their own words.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
